      "authorImage": "https://yt3.ggpht.com/gQVa7Y-7zD2So_SFNM5CopHBTmlsTw4fa1ZZGHuThY4CTMIWyUyz5qDuOs7pWLLwr8eS-eKhPA=s48-c-k-c0x00ffffff-no-rj",
      "text": "The interplay between scope clauses and fleet economics is the part most people never hear about — labor contracts quietly dictating airframe design, not the other way around. The 50-seat jet section really drives home how an optimization problem with thousands of constraints (range, crew costs, maintenance commonality, airport fees) converges on a handful of winners. It also explains why the A320/737 duopoly is so entrenched: once an airline commits to pilot training and spares for one type, the switching costs become enormous. This is basically infrastructure lock-in theory applied to aviation.",
